{
  "dom_id": "step-q133941",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14227574/assets/2130538a141293fda6b1bf07c1f0e8e5f7c347a2c688074689b1c13583a11bd2.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points. Estimate the value of $y$ when $x=20.$ \n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear, and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,23)$ and $(9,27).$*",
  "worked_solution": "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14227574/assets/a00942c18663c8e9ea2dd2c5354bbe177f7f8b35a4568c5234b9c92b844f196a.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,23)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(9,27).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27-23}{9-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{8} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(1,23):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+b \\\\ 23 & =0.5(1)+b \\\\ 23 & =0.5+b \\\\ b & =22.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=0.5x+22.5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=20$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+22.5 \\\\  & =0.5(20)+22.5 \\\\  & =10+22.5 \\\\  & =32.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=20$ we have $y≈32.5.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=20$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
